---
import Mark from "@guiferviz/cheese-bytes-video/astro/Mark.astro";
import StorySection from "@guiferviz/cheese-bytes-video/astro/StorySection.astro";
import VideoVisual from "@guiferviz/cheese-bytes-video/astro/VideoVisual.astro";
import {
  MajorityVideoVisual,
  majorityVideoDefinition,
} from "../components/cave/majority-vote/video/MajorityVideoVisual";
---

<StorySection
  id="stream"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "start",
      to: "question",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 4,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />Imagine a billion shapes passing right in front of you.
    </p>
    <p><Mark id="shapes" />Squares, circles, triangles, diamonds.</p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="majority" />Somewhere in this endless stream, one shape appears
      more often than all the others combined.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="question" />But which one? And more importantly, how could we
      find it?
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "stream", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="counting"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "count",
      to: "simple",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 4,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p><Mark id="start" />Well, the obvious solution is to count.</p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="count" />Every time we see a square, we increase its counter.
      Every circle, another counter. Every triangle, another.
    </p>
    <p><Mark id="simple" />Simple, right? But there's a problem.</p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "counting", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="memory"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.setSeed, { at: "start", params: { seed: 42 } });
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "start",
      to: "question",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 2,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />What if we're not dealing with four different shapes,
      but millions of different values?
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="values" />User IDs, IP addresses, or any other kind of data.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="many" />Suddenly, we need millions of counters, and if the data
      is large enough, we might not even have enough memory to store them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="question" />So here's a question. What if we could find the
      majority without remembering how many times every shape appeared?
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "memory", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="cancel"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "pair-one",
      duration: 0.8,
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 1,
      },
    });
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "pair-two",
      duration: 0.8,
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 2,
      },
    });
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "repeat",
      to: "left",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 4,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />Let's forget about computers for a moment. Imagine ten
      shapes. Six squares and four other shapes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="game" />Now, let's play a little game. Pick any two different
      shapes and remove them.
    </p>
    <p><Mark id="pair-one" />A square and a circle? Gone.</p>
    <p><Mark id="pair-two" />A square and a triangle? Gone.</p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="repeat" />Keep doing this until you can't remove any more pairs.
    </p>
    <p><Mark id="left" />What's left? Squares.</p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "cancel", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="invariant"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />And here's the interesting part. It doesn't matter how
      we choose the pairs. As long as squares make up more than half of the
      original shapes, some squares must survive.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="why" />Because there simply aren't enough other shapes to cancel
      them all.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="trick" />That's it. That's the entire trick. A true majority
      cannot be completely cancelled out by different elements.
    </p>
    <p><Mark id="catch" />But there's a catch.</p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "invariant", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="online"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "first",
      duration: 0.8,
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 1,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />So far, we've had all our shapes in front of us. What
      happens when they arrive one at a time?
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="memory" />We can't keep all those shapes around just to pair
      them later. That would bring us right back to our memory problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="two" />Fortunately, we don't have to. Instead, we only need to
      remember two things: a candidate and its strength.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="first" />The first shape becomes our candidate with a strength
      of one.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "online", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="algorithm"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "match",
      duration: 0.8,
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 2,
      },
    });
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "new",
      duration: 0.8,
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 3,
      },
    });
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "all",
      to: "guarantee",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 10,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />When another shape arrives, we compare it with our
      candidate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="match" />If they match, we increase the strength. If they're
      different, we decrease it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="cancel" />Think of that decrease as cancelling one copy of each
      shape.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="zero" />And when the strength reaches zero, we've cancelled
      everything we were keeping track of.
    </p>
    <p><Mark id="new" />The next shape can become our new candidate.</p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="all" />That's all. We process every element once without storing
      the entire stream.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="guarantee" />If a majority exists, our final candidate is
      guaranteed to be that majority.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "algorithm", progress: 1 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="complexity"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p><Mark id="start" />This is the Boyer-Moore Majority Vote Algorithm.</p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="cost" />It takes linear time and uses constant extra memory.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="two" />Just one candidate and one counter. Pretty amazing, isn't
      it?
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "complexity", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="catch"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "candidate",
      to: "survive",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 7,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />But wait. What if no shape appears more than half the
      time?
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="candidate" />Well, the algorithm might still return a candidate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="survive" />Remember, surviving the cancellations doesn't
      necessarily mean having a majority.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="only" />It only means that the candidate wasn't completely
      cancelled out.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "catch", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="verify"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "start",
      to: "end",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 2,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />So, unless we already know a majority exists, we need a
      second pass to verify the result.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "verify", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="distributed"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "summaries",
      duration: 0.8,
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 1,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />And now, here's where things get even more interesting.
      Let's go back to our billion data points.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="three" />But this time, imagine they're split across three
      different machines.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="summaries" />Each machine runs exactly the same process and
      sends back just two things: its candidate and its strength.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "distributed", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="merge"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
    t.action(visual.advance, {
      from: "start",
      to: "combine",
      ease: "linear",
      params: {
        value: 1,
      },
    });
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />Then we apply that same cancellation logic to those
      summaries.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="combine" />And just like that, we've handled a billion elements
      without ever building a giant frequency table.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "merge", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<StorySection
  id="closing"
  timeline={(t) => {
    const visual = t.visual("majority", majorityVideoDefinition);
  }}
>
  <aside class="notes">
    <p>
      <Mark id="start" />That's why this idea shows up in big data contexts: one
      pass, tiny memory, easy to combine.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Mark id="check" />So yes, it's a beautiful trick, provided we remember
      when we still need that final check.
    </p>
    <Mark id="end" />
  </aside>
  <VideoVisual id="majority">
    <MajorityVideoVisual
      initial={{ scene: "closing", progress: 0 }}
      client:only="react"
    />
  </VideoVisual>
</StorySection>

<style is:global>
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Majority Iosevka";
    src: url("/fonts/IosevkaTermSlabNerdFontPropo-Regular.woff2")
      format("woff2");
    font-weight: 400;
    font-display: block;
  }
  [data-majority-scene] {
    font-family: "Majority Iosevka", monospace !important;
  }
</style>
